    "Construction & Trades",
    "Logistics & Transport",
    "Education & Training",
    "Retail & E-commerce",
  ],
  faqs: [
    {
      question: "How much does software maintenance cost per year?",
      answer: "A common planning figure is 15 to 20% of the original build cost each year, covering patches, upgrades, bug fixes and small changes, plus hosting and licences on top. The real figure depends on how many dependencies and integrations the system has, how often it changes, and what response times you need. Our software maintenance cost guide explains the drivers and the arithmetic in more detail, and the retainer ranges on this page show typical monthly figures.",
    },
    {
      question: "What is included in a maintenance retainer?",
      answer: "A retainer includes a monthly block of senior engineering hours, response targets by severity during covered hours, dependency and security patching on a regular cycle, monitoring and alerting, runtime and framework upgrades planned ahead of end-of-life, small improvements from your backlog, and a monthly report. Larger features and major upgrades are scoped and quoted separately so they do not quietly consume the retainer.",
    },
    {
      question: "Do you offer after-hours or 24/7 support?",
      answer: "Our standard cover is business hours, Australian Eastern time, Monday to Friday. For systems where an outage at night or on a weekend has real cost, we offer an on-call option for critical incidents, priced separately because it means an engineer is rostered and reachable outside hours. We will tell you honestly if your system does not need it.",
    },
    {
      question: "Can you take over software another company built?",
      answer: "Yes. We start with a paid handover and health check rather than an immediate retainer, because we need to confirm we can build, deploy and safely change the system before we commit to response targets. If the original vendor has disappeared or will not cooperate, we work from the source code and accounts you own and document anything that is missing, such as credentials or undocumented infrastructure.",
    },
    {
      question: "What happens to unused hours?",
      answer: "Unused hours in a month go to your backlog of small improvements, technical debt reduction or documentation, agreed with you in advance. We do not bank hours indefinitely, because a large accrued balance usually means the retainer is set too high, and we would rather recommend a lower tier.",
    },
    {
      question: "Why do dependency updates matter if the software works fine?",
      answer: "Working software can still be vulnerable. Security issues are regularly found and published in open source libraries, and once a fix is public, attackers know exactly what to look for in unpatched systems. Runtimes also reach end of life on published dates, after which they stop receiving security fixes. Small regular upgrades are cheap and low risk; large overdue ones are expensive and risky.",
    },
    {
      question: "Do we lose control of our code by using a retainer?",
      answer: "No. The code stays in your repository and your cloud accounts stay in your name. We work through your access, commit through normal pull requests and keep runbooks up to date, so another team or your own staff can take over at any time. We will assist with the transition if you decide to move maintenance elsewhere.",
    },
  ],
  comparison: {
    rightFor: [
      "You have a live application that customers or staff depend on, and no in-house engineer whose job includes patching and upgrades",
      "Your software was built by a vendor or developer who is no longer available, and you need someone to take responsibility for it",
      "You need written response targets for incidents, for your own peace of mind or because a customer contract asks for them",
      "The system has several dependencies, integrations or runtimes that need planned upgrades before they reach end of life",
      "You want a steady flow of small improvements without scoping a new project each time",
    ],
    notRightFor: [
      "You already employ engineers who own the system; ad hoc specialist help or a code audit may serve you better than a retainer",
      "The software is a simple brochure site or an off-the-shelf SaaS product, where the vendor or a basic hosting plan already covers maintenance",
      "The system is due to be retired or replaced within months, where minimal keep-alive support is more sensible than a full retainer",
      "You need a large new feature or a rebuild, which should be scoped as a project rather than squeezed into monthly hours",
      "You need a 24/7 staffed operations centre watching your systems around the clock, which is the domain of dedicated managed service providers",
    ],
  },
  pricing: [
    {
      name: "Essentials (typical range)",
      range: "$1.5k to $3.5k / month",
      detail: "Typical Australian market range, AUD ex GST, not a quote. About 1 to 2.5 senior days a month at a planning rate of roughly $1,400 a day: business-hours support, monthly patch cycle, dependency scanning, basic uptime and error monitoring, and a monthly report. Suits a single application with modest change.",
    },
    {
      name: "Standard (typical range)",
      range: "$4k to $9k / month",
      detail: "Typical range, AUD ex GST. About 3 to 6 senior days a month: faster response targets, planned runtime and framework upgrades, performance and cost reviews, and a regular backlog of small improvements. Suits a customer-facing product or a business-critical internal system.",
    },
    {
      name: "Priority with on-call (typical range)",
      range: "$10k to $25k+ / month",
      detail: "Typical range, AUD ex GST. More hours, rostered on-call for critical incidents outside business hours, and quarterly security and architecture reviews. A one-off handover and health check, typically $5k to $20k depending on system size, applies before any retainer for software we did not build.",
    },
  ],
  definitions: [
    {
      term: "Service level agreement (SLA)",
      definition: "The written terms that set out how quickly a support provider will respond to, and work on, issues of each severity, during which hours, and what happens if the targets are missed.",
    },
    {
      term: "Response time vs resolution time",
      definition: "Response time is how quickly an engineer acknowledges and starts work on an issue. Resolution time is how long it takes to fix it. Honest agreements commit to response times, because resolution depends on the problem.",
    },
    {
      term: "End of life (EOL)",
      definition: "The date after which a runtime, framework or product stops receiving updates, including security fixes. Software still running on an end-of-life component becomes steadily more exposed.",
    },
    {
      term: "Long-term support (LTS)",
      definition: "A release line that a project commits to supporting for an extended, published period. Node.js supports each LTS release for 30 months, which is why production systems should run on LTS versions.",
    },
    {
      term: "Dependency update",
      definition: "Moving a third-party library your code relies on to a newer version, usually to pick up security fixes or maintain compatibility. Tools such as Dependabot and Renovate propose these automatically for review.",
    },
    {
      term: "On-call roster",
      definition: "A schedule that makes a named engineer reachable outside business hours to respond to critical incidents, usually paid as a standby fee plus time worked when called.",
    },
    {
      term: "Runbook",
      definition: "A written, step-by-step procedure for a routine or emergency task, such as deploying, rolling back or restoring from a backup, so anyone on the team can do it correctly under pressure.",
    },
  ],
  relatedServices: [
    { title: "Software Maintenance Cost Guide", href: "/guides/software-maintenance-cost" },
